# Install-script policy.
#
# `allowScripts` in package.json is an allowlist of dependencies permitted to
# run install scripts. Without `strict-allow-scripts` it is only a warning: an
# unreviewed package runs its script and npm prints a notice. With it, an
# unreviewed package with an install script fails the install instead — which
# is what makes the allowlist a control rather than a comment.
#
# npm only enforces this setting from 11.x on (Node 22's bundled npm 10.9.9
# ignores it silently), so the toolchain floor is declared in package.json
# `engines` (node >=22.9.0, npm >=11.17.0), asserted exactly in CI before
# `npm ci`, and re-checked locally by `npm run check:runtime`.
#
# `engine-strict` is deliberately NOT set: the deploy platform installs the
# already-approved lockfile with its own bundled npm, and a hard npm floor
# there would break deploys without adding control.
#
# `npm ci` respects this, so a malicious or replaced transitive dependency that
# adds a `preinstall`/`postinstall` hook breaks CI rather than executing.
#
# Adding a package here is a review decision. Pin an exact version where the
# package runs a script; a bare name matches every version.
#
# Current entries and why each is trusted:
#   fsevents@2.3.3 / @2.3.2  macOS-only optional file-watcher backend pulled in
#                            by dev tooling (chokidar via next/vitest; playwright).
#                            Its install script is `node-gyp rebuild` — a local
#                            native build of the watcher binding on a macOS dev
#                            machine, from the pinned registry tarball. Optional
#                            and darwin-only, so Linux CI never installs it.
#   unrs-resolver@1.12.2     native resolver behind eslint-plugin-import-x; its
#                            postinstall (napi-postinstall) verifies/installs
#                            the platform-specific binding package.
#
# See https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/configuring-npm/package-json#allowscripts
strict-allow-scripts=true
